Chapter 5 — Part 1
Morning traffic moved slow toward the U.A. gates. Curtis had the clean uniform on, duffel lighter without the mask, braids tied back tight. He’d slept four hours in the booth and caught the early train. The low pull in his chest was still there, quiet as ever. He didn’t think about it.
The gates were crowded. Students in the same gray jackets funneled through in loose packs. Curtis kept to the edge of the flow, eyes on the main building, until a shoulder slammed hard into his.
Bakugo didn’t bounce off. He stopped, turned, and glared up at the height difference like it had personally offended him. Palms already crackling, small pops of sweat ignition he wasn’t bothering to hide.
“The hell are you walking like you own the damn path?” Bakugo’s voice cut sharp over the morning noise. “Exchange student. Yeah, I saw that throw yesterday. Don’t get cocky just ’cause Aizawa made you show off. You’re still an extra until you prove you ain’t gonna fold the second something real shows up.”
A few nearby students slowed. Midoriya was farther up the path, green hair visible, already half-turned with that worried look he got whenever Bakugo started. Uraraka stood near him, eyes flicking between them.
Curtis rolled the shoulder Bakugo had hit. No heat in his voice. “You bumped me. I was walking straight.”
“Don’t care.” Bakugo stepped in closer, explosions ticking louder. “Stay outta my way in class. And don’t think that mountain crap you told Aizawa makes you special. I don’t lose to people who hold back on purpose.”
Curtis met his eyes. The red one and the blue one under the morning light. “Wasn’t trying to beat you. Just did the test he asked for.”
Bakugo’s teeth showed. “Then don’t act like you’re above it. Next time we pair up, I’m not going easy. Got it?”
“Got it.”
Bakugo scoffed, shoved past him hard enough to rock the duffel, and stalked toward the building. The small crowd started moving again. Midoriya hesitated like he wanted to say something, then didn’t. Uraraka watched Curtis a second longer than the others, cheeks faintly pink, before she followed the flow.
Curtis adjusted the strap and kept walking. Gate cleared. Class ahead. Bakugo’s anger trailed behind him like smoke, already burning toward whatever came next. The quiet warmth in his chest didn’t shift. He still didn’t have a name for it.
Curtis watched Bakugo’s back disappear into the crowd, explosions still snapping at his sides like he needed the noise. A couple of students gave the tall exchange student a wider berth after the shove. Midoriya had already turned away, notebook half-raised like he was fighting the urge to write the whole thing down. Uraraka lingered one extra second, then hurried to catch up with Iida.
Curtis shook his head once and started walking again. The duffel settled against his hip. Morning light cut across the path between the gates and the main building.
“So insecure,” he muttered, accent low enough that only the nearest students might have caught it. “Sheesh. And I thought I had problems.”
He didn’t say it for anyone else. Just rolled his shoulder where Bakugo had hit him, checked that nothing was pulled, and kept moving toward Class 1-A. The gates fell behind him. Ahead, the building doors stood open and the day’s noise was already spilling out—Iida’s voice carrying about schedules, someone’s laugh, the ordinary scramble of a second day that still felt like the first for most of them.
The quiet pull in his chest stayed exactly where it had been since the hospital. Unnamed. Steady. He adjusted his grip on the strap and stepped through the doors with the rest of the flow, already thinking about where he’d sit and whether Aizawa would pretend the crater in the woods had never happened.
Curtis had just cleared the main doors when two voices caught him from the side.
“Hey, new guy! Wait up!”
Mina bounced into his path, pink skin bright under the hallway lights, horns catching the fluorescents. She grinned wide, hands planted on her hips like she’d already decided they were friends. Momo followed a step behind, posture straight, the usual calm on her face tempered by open curiosity. Both of them slowed when they got close enough to take in the full height and the braids.
Mina tilted her head. “You’re Beardy, right? Curtis? The one who yeeted that ball into next week yesterday. Bakugo’s still steaming about it in the group chat.” She laughed, easy. “I’m Mina. This is Momo. We sit up front most days. You heading to 1-A?”
“Yeah.” Curtis shifted the duffel so it wouldn’t clip either of them. His voice stayed level. “Curtis is fine.”
Momo offered a small, precise nod. “Yaoyorozu Momo. It’s good to properly meet you. Aizawa doesn’t usually single someone out for a second throw unless he’s measuring something specific. Your results were… notable.” She studied him a moment, analytical but not unkind. “If you need the layout of the building or the day’s schedule, I keep a copy. Most of us are still learning where everything is.”
Mina leaned in a little, grinning. “Or if you just want the shortcut to the vending machines that actually work. Momo’s the responsible one. I’m the one who knows which teachers pretend they don’t see you running in the halls.” Her eyes flicked over him again, playful. “You always this quiet, or is Bakugo just that good at killing the mood?”
Curtis’s mouth twitched. Almost a smile. “Little of both. He’ll cool off or he won’t. Not much I can do about it in a hallway.”
Mina snorted. “Fair. Come on, then. We’ll walk you in so Iida doesn’t try to assign you a seat before you even put your bag down.” She spun on her heel and started down the corridor, waving for them to follow. Momo fell in on Curtis’s other side, already pulling a slim notebook from her bag in case he actually wanted the schedule.
The three of them moved with the rest of the morning flow. Mina kept talking—light, quick, filling the space. Momo added the occasional useful detail. Curtis answered when it made sense and let the rest roll past him, boots steady on the tile while the classroom door got closer.
The classroom had barely settled when the door slid open again.
Aizawa stepped in first, capture weapon loose around his neck, expression already done with the morning. Nezu followed at his heel, small and precise in his suit, smile polite and sharp at the edges. The room went quiet fast. Even Bakugo stopped mid-mutter.
“Beardy,” Aizawa said. No preamble. “You skipped the entrance exam. Nezu cleared the transfer anyway. I argued. He won. That doesn’t mean you walk into my class without the same baseline everyone else had to show.”
Nezu’s ears twitched. He hopped lightly onto the teacher’s desk so he could see the room properly. “Mr. Aizawa raised several practical concerns. I agreed that a demonstration was reasonable. The standard villain bots used in the entrance exam should suffice.”
Curtis stayed in his seat near the back, duffel on the floor beside him. He looked between them, then shook his head once.
“Fighting those weak robots would be a waste of good class time.” His accent stayed flat. “Everybody here already broke them or watched somebody else do it. I can punch a one-pointer. Doesn’t tell you anything you didn’t get from the ball throw.”
A few students shifted. Mina’s eyebrows went up. Momo’s pencil paused over her notes. Bakugo scoffed under his breath.
Nezu’s smile widened, genuinely pleased. “I suspected you might say that.” He folded his small paws. “Very well. Adjusted parameters. You will face three zero-point bots. Simultaneously. One versus three. The same models that end the entrance exam. We will observe from the monitoring room. The exercise begins in twenty minutes on Ground Beta. Full hero course gear is not required. Your current uniform will do.”
Aizawa’s eyes stayed on Curtis. “You don’t get to sandbag this one. Those things are built to pressure a whole testing group. Three of them on one person is the measurement we actually need.”
Curtis rolled his shoulder—the same one Bakugo had hit at the gates—and stood. “Alright. Where do I change?”
“Locker room off the grounds,” Aizawa said. “Don’t make us wait.”
Nezu hopped down, still smiling. “I look forward to the data, Mr. Beardy. Do try to leave the arena standing. Reconstruction budgets are finite.”
The class stared. Mina was already grinning. Midoriya had his notebook open again, pencil moving. Curtis picked up his duffel, nodded once to both of them, and headed for the door while the room buzzed behind him. Three zero-pointers. One of him. Class time, apparently, was no longer being wasted.
The door shut behind Curtis. For a second the room just stared at it.
Mina broke first, twisting in her seat with a grin that was all teeth. “Three zero-pointers? By himself? Okay, that’s actually insane. I wanna see this.”
Kirishima slammed a fist into his open palm, hardening flickering over his knuckles. “Dude’s hardcore! Those things are huge—fighting three at once is manly as hell. Hope he doesn’t get wrecked.”
Bakugo’s palms popped. He was glaring at the door like it had insulted him. “Tch. Extra thinks he’s hot shit ’cause he threw a ball far. Those bots’ll fold him the second he stops holding back. Or they won’t. Either way I’m watching.”
Midoriya’s pencil was already flying across a fresh page, muttering under his breath. “Zero-pointers are built for area denial and raw mass, not precision. Three at once means overlapping attack patterns, debris, restricted movement—he’ll have to manage spacing or get pinned. If his baseline really is that high…” He trailed off, eyes wide, already sketching rough bot silhouettes.
Iida’s hand chopped the air. “This is highly irregular! An exchange student being tested separately on the second day disrupts the class schedule. However—if Principal Nezu and Mr. Aizawa both approved it, we should observe and take detailed notes for our own improvement!”
Uraraka had both hands pressed to her cheeks, floating a little in her seat without meaning to. “Three of them… that’s the same kind that almost squished me at the exam. He’s just walking out there like it’s normal.”
Momo closed her notebook with a soft click, expression thoughtful. “Aizawa wouldn’t allow it if he believed Mr. Beardy had no chance. Still, the zero-pointers are designed to overwhelm groups. One person changes the variables considerably. I’ll be interested in how he prioritizes targets.”
Jiro’s earjacks twitched. She leaned back, half-smiling. “Bet he makes it look boring on purpose. Guy barely reacted when Bakugo shoulder-checked him.”
Kaminari laughed, nervous and loud. “If he actually wins I’m never talking trash about exchange students again. Those things are nightmares.”
Todoroki sat with his arms crossed, heterochromatic eyes on the door, saying nothing. The temperature around his desk dipped a fraction before it evened out.
Aizawa pinched the bridge of his nose. “Enough. You all have ten minutes to reach the observation deck on Ground Beta if you want to watch. Anyone who treats this like free entertainment instead of a lesson can run laps after. Move.”
Chairs scraped. The class spilled into the hallway in a rush of voices and hurried footsteps, already arguing about angles and outcomes while Curtis changed somewhere ahead of them.
Tsuyu’s finger rested against her cheek as the class spilled into the hall. “Ribbit. Three zero-pointers at once is a lot of mass to manage. If he gets cornered he’ll have trouble. I hope he has a plan.”
Sero walked backward a few steps so he could face the group, tape dispensers already flexing. “Dude better not get launched into the stands. Those things hit like trucks. I’m bringing popcorn energy either way.”
Tokoyami’s cloak shifted, Dark Shadow peeking out with a low chuckle. “A solitary stand against overwhelming force. How dramatic. We shall see whether the exchange student possesses the resolve to match the spectacle.”
Shoji’s extra arms folded as he moved with the crowd. “Zero-pointers prioritize destruction over capture. Spacing will matter more than raw power once all three are active. I’ll be watching how he repositions.”
Ojiro’s tail swayed behind him, expression thoughtful. “He didn’t look worried. Either he’s confident or he’s never seen one of those things move up close. Guess we’ll find out.”
Mineta was already sweating, grape balls popping nervously from his head. “Three of them?! He’s gonna get pasted! Why would anyone agree to that on purpose? I’m staying at the back of the deck. Way at the back.”
Aoyama struck a brief pose in the middle of the hallway, sparkles somehow already present. “Such courage! To face the abyss alone… how magnifique. I shall observe from a safe and well-lit angle, of course.”
Hagakure’s gloves waved as she bounced along. “This is gonna be crazy! Do you think he’ll actually beat them? I kinda hope he does. It’d be so cool.”
Koda walked quietly near the edge of the group, hands clasped, watching the others more than the path. He didn’t speak, but his eyes stayed wide.
Sato rubbed the back of his neck, already thinking about the sugar he’ll need later. “Those bots are tough. If he pulls it off I’m buying him a pastry. If he doesn’t… well, Recovery Girl’s probably on standby anyway.”
The class kept moving, voices overlapping all the way toward Ground Beta. Ahead of them, somewhere in the locker room, Curtis was already pulling on the spare gym clothes Aizawa had left out. The observation deck was filling. The zero-pointers were being moved into position. Class time, apparently, had other plans.
The monitoring room overlooked Ground Beta from a reinforced booth. Screens showed every angle of the fake city—empty streets, half-collapsed storefronts, the usual training debris. Present Mic spun in the spare chair, headphones around his neck, grinning like he’d just pulled off a heist.
“Hey, Mr. Aizawa, guess what? I put some sweet battle music on for the 1v3!” He slapped the console. Bass already thumped through the external speakers below, low and building. “Gotta have the right atmosphere, yeah? Can’t have the new kid fighting giant robots in dead silence. That’s just sad!”
Aizawa didn’t look up from the main feed. “Turn it off.”
“Aw, come on, it’s cinematic! Nezu approved the extra speakers last semester—”
“Turn it off before I erase your vocal cords for the afternoon.”
Present Mic laughed, loud and unbothered, but his hand hovered over the volume. “You’re no fun. Fine, fine. But when the bots drop I’m fading it back in. For the drama.”
Down on the ground, Curtis stood in the middle of a cracked intersection. Gym clothes. No gear. Braids tied tight. The fake city stretched around him—silent except for the faint hum of distant machinery and the low pulse of music that hadn’t quite died yet. No bots in sight. Just empty streets and the long shadows of the training buildings.
He rolled his neck once. Waited. The observation deck above was already dotted with Class 1-A faces pressed to the glass. He didn’t look up.
Somewhere under the city, heavy treads began to turn. The music cut. Aizawa’s voice came through the ground speakers, flat and clear.
“Beardy. They’re released. Don’t make me regret this.”
Curtis set his feet. The first distant crash echoed between the buildings. Then another. Then a third.
He smiled, small and sharp.
“Alright then.”
The first zero-pointer came around the corner already at full charge, treads chewing pavement, optics locked. It didn’t slow. The massive fist cocked back and dropped like a wrecking ball aimed straight at Curtis’s head.
He slammed both feet into the concrete. Cracks spiderwebbed out from his boots. His hands shot up and caught the descending fist dead-center, arms locking, shoulders braced. Metal screamed. The impact drove him down an inch—then stopped. The bot’s entire forward momentum hung on his grip.
Curtis looked up into the glowing sensors, voice low and even.
“Your heaps of scrap are nothing special. Let me show you what happens when you face something that’s tasted death more than once.”
He twisted. Servos shrieked. The giant arm tore free at the shoulder joint in a shower of sparks and hydraulic fluid. Curtis didn’t pause. He pivoted, swung the severed limb like a bat, and launched it upward. The arm spun end over end, then crashed straight down through the bot’s head. Armor caved. Optics burst. The whole machine staggered, listed, and collapsed onto its side in a grinding heap of ruined plating.
Dust rolled across the intersection.
One down.
The other two were already closing in from opposite streets, heavier footsteps shaking the fake city. Curtis rolled his wrists, shook concrete dust off his palms, and set his feet again. The observation deck had gone loud. He didn’t look up.
“Two to go.”
The remaining two zero-pointers didn’t wait. Missile pods snapped open along their shoulders. A volley dropped straight down the intersection—bright trails, sharp whistles, impacts that turned the street into a cloud of fire and shattered concrete. Right behind the blasts the bots charged, massive fists already swinging, trying to crush whatever was left in the smoke.
On the observation deck several students flinched. Mina slapped both hands over her eyes. Mineta yelped and ducked behind Sero. Even Kirishima winced. Midoriya’s pencil froze halfway across the page.
The dust thinned.
Curtis stood in the middle of the cratered street, completely upright. One hand had lifted to idly twist the end of a braid between his fingers. Concrete dust coated his gym clothes. A few shallow cuts marked his arms. He looked at the two bots still rolling toward him and shook his head.
“Accuracy’s terrible. You’re shooting at a stationary target and still missing the important parts.” He flicked the braid back over his shoulder. “Aw, who cares. You lame robots can’t understand me anyway.”
He beat a fist twice against his chest. The sound carried—solid, rhythmic, like a drum.
“Beat loudly, heart. Beat loud.”
Then he dropped into a stance, feet set, fists raised, weight balanced the way someone stands when they’ve decided the fight is already simple.
“Let’s box this out, you giant bags of sheet metal.”
He moved.
The bots’ optics couldn’t track him. One second he was in front of the left machine; the next his fist had already punched a clean hole through its torso plating, sparks and fluid spraying. He slipped the wild counter-swing, appeared at the second bot’s flank, and drove another punch straight through its hip joint. Metal folded. He didn’t stop. A third strike caved in a knee. A fourth tore through an elbow actuator. Every hit landed exact, too fast for the machines to reset their guard.
Curtis planted, then launched himself forward and simply ran through both of them.
His body hit the first bot like a battering ram, armor shrieking as he tore straight through its midsection and out the other side. Momentum carried him into the second. He crashed through that one too—shoulder, ribs, hips—ripping a jagged tunnel of ruined plating and severed cables before he burst clear on the far side.
The two zero-pointers collapsed inward, frames buckling, and crashed down in a single grinding pile of scrap.
Dust rolled out and slowly settled.
When it cleared, Curtis was sitting on top of the wreckage, one knee up, casually humming under his breath. He flicked a piece of shrapnel off his thigh and glanced toward the observation deck like he was checking whether class was dismissed yet.
The monitoring room was dead silent except for the soft beep of Aizawa’s tablet recording the last of the data.
The last of the dust drifted across the main screen. Curtis sat on the scrap pile below, still humming, completely unbothered.
Present Mic had both hands on his head. “YEAHHH! Did you SEE that?! He just—he ran through them! Like they were paper! That’s not normal enhancer output, that’s—that’s a whole highlight reel! I’m putting this on the radio tonight, I don’t care what anyone says!”
Aizawa stared at the feed, capture weapon perfectly still. His eyes were narrow. “He didn’t use a single visible technique. No bracing past the first catch. No wasted movement. He treated three zero-pointers like a conditioning drill.” He exhaled through his nose. “Nezu.”
Nezu was already stepping down from his chair, small paws folded behind his back, smile perfectly satisfied. “I did tell you the standard exam bots would be insufficient. Mr. Beardy has spent years treating far less predictable threats as routine. A machine that telegraphs every strike was never going to trouble him.” He paused at the door, ears twitching with quiet amusement. “Told you so, Mr. Aizawa.”
Aizawa didn’t answer. He just locked the tablet and watched Curtis hop down from the wreckage like he’d finished a light warm-up. Present Mic was still yelling about angles and instant replays. Nezu slipped out of the monitoring room, already composing the short, classified note he would add to the exchange student’s file before the hour was done.
Down on the field, Curtis rolled his shoulder once, brushed dust off his shirt, and started walking toward the exit tunnel. The observation deck above him had finally found its voice again.
The deck exploded the second the dust thinned enough to see Curtis sitting on the scrap pile.
Mina had both hands on the glass, mouth open. “He just—he ran through them! Like, through them! That’s not fair, that’s crazy!”
Kirishima was grinning so hard his sharpening teeth showed. “That was the manliest thing I’ve ever seen! He didn’t even look tired! I gotta train harder—way harder!”
Bakugo’s palms crackled hard enough to scorch the railing. “What the hell?! He was holding back yesterday?! Those things are supposed to wreck whole groups and he treated them like punching bags—damn extra—!”
Midoriya’s notebook was already filled with frantic new lines, muttering at full speed. “Clean penetration strikes, no visible quirk activation past baseline, he managed spacing against two simultaneous heavy units without ever getting pinned—his physical output is way beyond a standard enhancer, this rewrites the whole threat assessment—”
Iida’s engines revved in pure agitation. “Such overwhelming power displayed with zero regard for the structural integrity of the training ground! We must document every detail for future reference! Also someone should check if he requires medical attention!”
Uraraka floated a few inches off the floor without noticing, hands on her cheeks. “He was just… sitting there humming. After all that. I think I’m gonna pass out.”
Momo’s eyes were sharp, already analyzing. “He prioritized disabling mobility and structural points before the final charge. Extremely efficient. If that level of output is his normal ceiling, group exercises will need to be completely rebalanced.”
Jiro’s earjacks were plugged into the railing from the bass of the impacts. She winced, then snorted. “Guy fights giant robots and then fixes his hair. Of course he does.”
Kaminari was laughing in disbelief. “I’m never making fun of exchange students again. Ever. Did you see him catch that first punch? Insane!”
Todoroki stood with his arms crossed, the temperature around him perfectly even. “…He’s strong. Stronger than the file suggested.”
Tsuyu’s head tilted. “Ribbit. He made it look easy. That’s scarier than if he’d struggled.”
Sero whistled low. “Tape’s not stopping that. Nothing we have is stopping that. Dude’s a monster.”
Tokoyami’s cloak rustled. “A decisive and unceremonious end. The darkness of those machines was no match for raw, practiced force. Impressive.”
Mineta was pale and gripping the back of someone’s jacket. “We’re in class with that?! I’m transferring. Right now. Somebody transfer me.”
The rest of the class pressed closer to the glass, voices overlapping, already arguing about what they’d just watched while Curtis hopped down from the pile below and started walking toward the exit like the whole thing had been a light spar.
